In article <2123@texsun.Central.Sun.COM>, andy@misha.Central.Sun.COM writes:
> Also,  pl7 seems to have tightened the reign on #pray,  before
> when I would pray (with < 5 hp or weak,  a previously happy god,
> and > 300 turns since last pray) it was usually successful.  Is
> this true ?

A while back, someone posted a chart showing how many turns you must
wait before it is safe to pray.  It was based on the rnd function, and
it showed that you would have to wait close to 1000 turns to have a
good chance (>75% or so) to pray successfully.

This seems too arbitrary.  Under the same circumstances, sometimes the
gods will like you, sometimes they will curse you.  The result is
usually that they lose worshippers.  After all, why pray to someone
who is so unpredictable?

So how about some changes to this part of the prayer code.  Time
between prayers should be based on what you do in the dungeon.  The
base time should be a function of experience points since the last
prayer, possibly normalized to the experience level of the player.
This base time can then be modified by such factors as:  dungeon
level, luck, current hps, current health, hunger status, alignment of
monsters killed, etc.

Now, the gods are probably busy people, which is why they don't want
to hear from you too often, but at certain times they are do want to
hear from you.  While wandering around the dungeon, you might get the
message "You feel like someone is watching you."  This means your god
is ready to hear a prayer.  But if you don't pray in some short amount
of time (say 5-10 turns), the god's attention will wander, and only at
this point does the chance of praying successfully have a random
element introduced.

Finally, if you're really in bad trouble (<5 hps, weak from hunger, as
now), you can risk praying early.  Success will depend on the time
calculated as above, modified for your current situation, with a
random factor thrown in.

This isn't much different than the way things work now, but it is less
arbitrary, and makes the gods just a little more reasonable.

